[00:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hi, I\'m Yevgeny, CEO of Splatiga. Let me tell you what Splatiga is. Think about how we used to take over the world. First it was photos, then videos, then 360° images. But all these formats have one'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'limitation. They are flat. You can look at a place, but you can\'t really get into it. At Splatiga, we are changing that. We take 360° video captured by a camera or drone and transform it into a photorealistic 3D'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment using Gaussian Splatting technology. So instead of spending days or weeks manually modeling a space , we simply capture a'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'real-world location, upload the footage, and Splatiga reconstructs it as an interactive 3D scene. And once that scene exists, it becomes much bigger than a video. You can explore'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it in your browser. You can enjoy it in virtual reality. You can render it into cinematic content. You can export it to professional pipelines for Unity, Unreal Engine, Isaac Sim, and other'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'digital twin workflows. This is important because almost every industry works with real spaces . Real estate needs better ways to showcase properties. Tourism needs more'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engaging ways to preview destinations. Filmmaking needs access to virtual locations. Cultural heritage needs tools to preserve places before they disappear. And robotics needs a realistic'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment where machines can be tested, trained, and prepared before they enter the physical world. That\'s why Splat is so important. We make spatial'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capture faster, more accessible, and more scalable. We are moving from flat media to spatial media, from viewing places on a screen to entering them as if they were digital worlds. You can visit splatit.com to'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see what thousands of our users have already created.'
